faq:
  - q: "What are the dominant industries in Atlanta and Georgia?"
    a: "Atlanta is the economic capital of the Southeast and one of the most dynamic job markets in the US. Dominant sectors: (1) Finance and Insurance — Atlanta is the global headquarters of several major financial institutions. Delta Community Credit Union, Fiserv Atlanta, Equifax, NCR Voyix (now Atleos), SunTrust (now Truist), and Invesco are headquartered here. The FinTech sector is particularly strong — Atlanta processes roughly 70% of the world's credit card transactions, earning the nickname 'Transaction Alley.' (2) Technology — Google's Atlanta office, Microsoft, Salesforce, and dozens of enterprise software and SaaS companies have significant Atlanta operations. The tech startup ecosystem is centered around Tech Square near Georgia Tech. (3) Media and Entertainment — Georgia's generous film tax credits have made Atlanta one of the leading film and TV production markets in the US. Tyler Perry Studios, Pinewood Atlanta Studios, and hundreds of productions have operated in Georgia. (4) Logistics and Transportation — Atlanta's Hartsfield-Jackson airport (the world's busiest) and Delta Air Lines headquarters make logistics and transportation major employment sectors. UPS is headquartered in Sandy Springs. (5) Healthcare — Emory Healthcare, Piedmont Healthcare, and the CDC headquarters make healthcare a dominant Atlanta employer."
  - q: "What is Atlanta's FinTech ecosystem and how do you break into it?"
    a: "Atlanta's FinTech ecosystem is one of the most significant in the US, built on the payment processing infrastructure that processes the majority of US card transactions. Key companies: NCR Voyix, Global Payments, Fiserv (Atlanta operations), Worldpay, Cardlytics, Kabbage (now American Express), and dozens of payment technology companies. Breaking into Atlanta FinTech: (1) Georgia Tech is the primary talent pipeline for Atlanta FinTech engineering roles — CS and electrical engineering from GT is highly valued in this ecosystem. (2) The Atlanta FinTech community is relationship-driven — Atlanta Tech Village, ATDC (Advanced Technology Development Center), and the Atlanta FinTech Collective are key networking venues. (3) For business and product roles: fintech business knowledge (payment rails, card network economics, acquiring and issuing) is more important than generic tech product experience. Learn the domain before interviewing. (4) Compensation in Atlanta FinTech is below SF and NYC fintech equivalents but significantly above Atlanta's cost of living — real purchasing power is high for Atlanta FinTech professionals."
  - q: "How does Georgia's film and entertainment job market work?"
    a: "Georgia's film industry has grown dramatically since the state introduced generous production tax credits. Key facts: (1) Scope — Georgia is now the third-largest film and TV production market in the US, behind California and New York. Major productions (Avengers, The Walking Dead, Stranger Things, Ozark) have filmed in Georgia. (2) Union membership matters for below-the-line roles — IATSE, IBEW, and Teamsters locals in Georgia represent grip, electric, camera, and transportation crew. Union membership or the ability to get on the union roster is critical for sustained crew employment. (3) Business and production management roles — production accountants, line producers, production coordinators, and VFX supervisors have strong demand in Atlanta's film ecosystem. (4) The ecosystem fluctuates with production volume — Georgia film employment is less stable than finance or tech, with production booms and slowdowns creating variable employment. (5) Networking is through the Georgia Production Partnership (GPP) and Atlanta Film Society."
  - q: "What is the Atlanta tech startup ecosystem like?"
    a: "Atlanta's tech startup ecosystem is centered around Tech Square, adjacent to Georgia Tech in Midtown Atlanta. Key characteristics: (1) Georgia Tech is the primary talent engine — GT's computer science, electrical engineering, and industrial engineering programs produce a continuous talent pipeline for Atlanta tech startups and established companies. (2) ATDC (Advanced Technology Development Center) at Georgia Tech is one of the most active startup incubators in the Southeast — startups here have early access to GT talent and research. (3) Stages: Atlanta has a stronger seed and Series A community than earlier in the decade; Kabbage's exit to American Express and Mailchimp's acquisition by Intuit generated significant founder wealth that has recirculated into Atlanta's startup ecosystem. (4) Notable companies: Calendly, Salesloft (now Drift), Terminus, OneTrust, and NCR Voyix's spin-offs have built significant Atlanta tech companies. (5) Compensation is below SF and NYC tech but purchasing power in Atlanta is among the highest in tech markets — lower cost of living, lower taxes (Georgia flat rate of 5.49%), and no city income tax."
  - q: "What career coaching do Atlanta professionals need?"
    a: "Atlanta-specific career coaching should address: (1) FinTech positioning — Atlanta's dominant industry requires domain knowledge that candidates from other markets frequently lack. Coaching that helps Atlanta job seekers develop payment technology fluency and position it in interviews is highly valuable. (2) Corporate relocation navigation — Atlanta has attracted significant corporate relocations and expansions (Microsoft, Apple, Google growth). Candidates joining these expansions from other markets need help understanding Atlanta's different professional culture and networking ecosystem. (3) Southeast professional networking — Atlanta's professional community is relationship-driven and requires in-person presence at key venues. Coaching on Atlanta-specific networking strategy (Atlanta Committee for Progress, Young Atlanta Chamber, Georgia Tech alumni network) is more valuable than generic LinkedIn advice. (4) Salary benchmarking in the Atlanta context — Georgia has lower compensation than coastal markets but strong purchasing power; coaching that helps Atlanta professionals understand market rates and negotiate effectively within the Atlanta context is critical."
